User: What is the relationship between water activity levels and mold growth in stored grains?
Assistant: Water activity (aw) is the primary factor controlling mold growth in stored grains, with most storage fungi requiring aw values above 0.70 to proliferate. Xerophilic species like Aspergillus restrictus can grow at aw as low as 0.70-0.75, while more common storage fungi such as A. flavus and Penicillium species typically require 0.80-0.85. The relationship follows a predictable pattern: below 0.70 aw, fungal growth is essentially prevented; between 0.70-0.80, only specialized xerophiles grow slowly; above 0.80, growth rates increase exponentially with each 0.05 increment. At 25°C, grain equilibrates to approximately 0.70 aw at 13.5% moisture content for wheat and 14% for corn. Temperature modulates these thresholds—higher temperatures generally increase minimum aw requirements. The practical implication is that maintaining grain moisture below 14% (corresponding to aw < 0.70) effectively prevents mold growth under most storage conditions, though localized moisture migration can create microenvironments exceeding these thresholds.

