User: What determines the effectiveness of pasteurization across different dairy product types?
Assistant: Pasteurization effectiveness depends on achieving specific time-temperature combinations that ensure adequate microbial reduction while minimizing product degradation. The standard HTST process (72°C for 15 seconds) achieves a 5-log reduction in pathogenic bacteria for fluid milk, but product composition significantly affects heat transfer and microbial thermal resistance. Higher fat content in cream requires adjusted parameters due to fat globules protecting microorganisms and impeding heat penetration. Solids content affects thermal conductivity - concentrated products like evaporated milk need higher temperatures or extended holding times. pH also matters: acidified products show enhanced microbial inactivation at lower temperatures due to synergistic effects. Particle size in products like chocolate milk or strawberry milk necessitates higher temperatures (typically 79°C) to ensure adequate treatment of suspended solids. The evidence shows that properly calibrated pasteurization reduces pathogenic bacteria to below detectable levels while preserving 95% of nutritional value. Validation requires demonstrating a 5-log reduction of Coxiella burnetii, the most heat-resistant pathogen of public health concern in milk.

